===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# committing [[sacrilege]]
# acting or speaking very disrespectfully toward what is held to be [[sacred]]

===Origin===
Owing to the phonetic similarities between the words sacrilegious and religious, and their spiritually-based uses in modern English, many people mistakenly assume that the two words are etymologically linked, or that one is an antonym of the other. Religious is derived from the Latin word religio, meaning "reverence, religion," whereas sacriligous is derived ultimately from the Latin combining form sacr-, meaning sacred, and the verb legere, meaning "to steal". The Latin noun sacrilegus means "one who steals sacred things". [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sacrilege#Origin]

===Trivia===
Sacrilegious was the winning word at the 18th Scripps National Spelling Bee. [http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Scripps_National_Spelling_Bee]

